lways sick i find them  sleeping all the time  when i ask them what's up they say mommy  my head mommy my head  but we don't have money to buy medicine  or even money to take them to hospital  this is the fifth time they have been  displaced due to flooding  it seems to be a never-ending cycle and  it seems little is being done to prevent  it  the only thing that could help dorothy  and the others in the long term would be  to build a dam  she recalls a visit by a government  minister  you know  minister eugene vamaya came to the camp  by helicopter he said they would release  money to build this dam  so when the water comes this way it  won't come back to our land and displace  us  but they have done nothing now  we take dorothy's statement to the  minister in nairobi  so what is your reaction to that yes we  have had this problem for quite a while  we have the long-term solution to this  which is really building a dam that  would cost to think over 20 billion it's  going to be a huge dam this  administration has actually prioritized  it and i'm very happy that  before the end of this year we should be  able to get it underway  he expects it to be completed in three  to four years and he promises cash  payments for the vulnerable  but elizabeth erbono doesn't believe  that she wants to know why it has taken  the government so long to act  she'd like to move to a safer area to  provide her children with a stable home  if you don't have money you have to  stick to the place that's yours no  matter how bad it is because you have no  means to go and rent a house  so we are forced to die in this place no  matter how bad it is  all she has is the hope that the  minister is true to his word  joining me now is the executive director  of the pan-african climate justice  alliance dr mithika wenda hello and  welcome to the program dr muenda now we  just saw a report about western kenya's  nyanza area facing constant flooding and  residents believe it's getting worse due  to c